WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:05.258
In this secondary substation, the medium-voltage switchgear is on the left,

2
00:00:05.479 --> 00:00:07.151
the transformer on the right.

3
00:00:08.219 --> 00:00:11.865
Switchgear switches, protects and isolates parts of the grid.

4
00:00:12.609 --> 00:00:17.996
Here it is an RMU, a ring main unit: one compact block in a sealed steel tank.

5
00:00:19.057 --> 00:00:22.900
It has three panels: one cabinet per cable or transformer.

6
00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:27.908
Two panels link the station into the ring; the third feeds the transformer.

7
00:00:28.649 --> 00:00:33.409
A line drawing on the front shows which switch is open, closed or earthed.

8
00:00:34.457 --> 00:00:36.524
Ask two things of every device.

9
00:00:37.386 --> 00:00:39.592
What can it make, and what can it break?

10
00:00:40.926 --> 00:00:41.982
In the substation,

11
00:00:42.169 --> 00:00:46.871
every medium-voltage feeder to a ring of stations starts at a circuit breaker.

12
00:00:47.895 --> 00:00:52.029
It switches load, but also breaks a short circuit, again and again,

13
00:00:52.226 --> 00:00:55.988
and is typically rated for 16 to 25 kiloamps.

14
00:00:57.102 --> 00:00:59.296
A protection relay measures the current.

15
00:01:00.072 --> 00:01:03.032
When it sees a fault, it tells the breaker to open.

16
00:01:04.093 --> 00:01:06.972
As the contacts part, an arc jumps across.

17
00:01:07.685 --> 00:01:11.226
In a vacuum interrupter it goes out at a current zero.

18
00:01:12.551 --> 00:01:13.143
How heavy?

19
00:01:13.854 --> 00:01:18.324
That depends on the fault level: how hard a short circuit hits at that point.

20
00:01:19.424 --> 00:01:24.752
Near a substation, in a 10 kilovolt grid, it is a few hundred megavolt-amperes,

21
00:01:24.904 --> 00:01:27.238
roughly 15 to 20 kiloamps.

22
00:01:28.253 --> 00:01:32.467
Every circuit breaker must be rated above the fault level where it sits.

23
00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:36.042
An example: a digger hits a ring cable.

24
00:01:36.727 --> 00:01:43.554
The relay sees 8 kiloamps, and the 20 kiloamp breaker opens in about 0.3 seconds.

25
00:01:44.863 --> 00:01:49.740
Back in the secondary substation, the two ring panels each have a load-break switch,

26
00:01:49.832 --> 00:01:52.491
for switching sections of the ring in and out.

27
00:01:53.588 --> 00:01:58.047
A load-break switch switches the normal load, and can close onto a fault,

28
00:01:58.209 --> 00:02:00.136
but cannot break a short circuit.

29
00:02:00.864 --> 00:02:04.103
So when the digger strikes, the breaker opens first.

30
00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:09.329
Then a technician opens the load-break switches around the damaged section,

31
00:02:09.515 --> 00:02:11.233
and the breaker goes back in.

32
00:02:12.202 --> 00:02:16.254
The third panel feeds the 400 kilovolt-ampere transformer,

33
00:02:16.393 --> 00:02:19.098
through a load-break switch and a fuse per phase.

34
00:02:20.144 --> 00:02:22.408
On a fault the fuse melts, once.

35
00:02:23.168 --> 00:02:24.782
Then a new one must go in.

36
00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.810
For work behind a panel there is the disconnector.

37
00:02:29.551 --> 00:02:33.069
You operate it only without current: it makes a safe gap.

38
00:02:34.131 --> 00:02:37.208
The earthing switch connects the dead section to earth.

39
00:02:37.938 --> 00:02:42.872
If voltage comes back by mistake, the current flows to earth, not through a person.

40
00:02:43.895 --> 00:02:48.899
In an RMU this is often one three-position switch: closed, open and earthed.

41
00:02:49.641 --> 00:02:54.296
An interlock blocks the wrong order, such as earthing a cable that is still live.

42
00:02:55.329 --> 00:02:57.372
Operating a disconnector under load,

43
00:02:57.546 --> 00:03:02.388
or closing a switch onto a fault it cannot handle, can cause an arc flash: heat,

44
00:03:02.562 --> 00:03:04.768
light and pressure that burn the operator.

45
00:03:05.475 --> 00:03:08.215
Good switchgear is tested against an internal

46
00:03:08.296 --> 00:03:11.187
arc and vents the blast away from the operator.

47
00:03:12.513 --> 00:03:16.368
A substation has a row of panels, each with a circuit breaker,

48
00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:21.719
measuring transformers that scale current and voltage down, and a protection relay.

49
00:03:22.711 --> 00:03:27.715
Air-insulated switchgear is large: air between the live parts insulates them,

50
00:03:27.819 --> 00:03:29.224
so it needs distance.

51
00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:34.796
Gas-insulated switchgear puts those parts in a sealed tank of SF6,

52
00:03:34.994 --> 00:03:38.012
an insulating gas, and is much more compact.

53
00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:42.066
Many RMUs are SF6-insulated too.

54
00:03:42.786 --> 00:03:47.686
Many stations still have older types, like the resin-insulated Magnefix,

55
00:03:47.837 --> 00:03:50.078
some with extra operating rules.

56
00:03:51.103 --> 00:03:54.400
A pressure gauge on the front shows the gas is still in.

57
00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:59.556
SF6 insulates and quenches arcs extremely well.

58
00:04:00.216 --> 00:04:07.182
But it is the strongest greenhouse gas in common use: about 23,500 times CO2,

59
00:04:07.286 --> 00:04:08.076
per kilogram.

60
00:04:09.057 --> 00:04:15.501
An example: 20,000 RMUs with 1.5 kilograms of gas each is 30 tonnes.

61
00:04:16.226 --> 00:04:20.592
If 0.1 percent leaks each year, that is 30 kilograms.

62
00:04:21.638 --> 00:04:28.569
Times 23,500, that is roughly 700 tonnes of CO2 equivalent, every year.

63
00:04:29.584 --> 00:04:32.045
So under the EU F-gas Regulation,

64
00:04:32.208 --> 00:04:39.917
new switchgear up to 24 kilovolts may in principle no longer use SF6 from 2026,

65
00:04:40.149 --> 00:04:43.748
and up to 52 kilovolts from 2030.

66
00:04:44.441 --> 00:04:49.805
New types break the arc in vacuum and insulate with dry air or solid materials.

67
00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.836
Switchgear stays 30 to 50 years, so the choice matters.

68
00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:57.408
Always ask: which type is it?

69
00:04:58.152 --> 00:05:02.099
The answer tells you how it is operated, maintained and replaced.

70
00:05:02.841 --> 00:05:04.699
And what stops someone using it wrong?

71
00:05:05.411 --> 00:05:07.814
Older units lack some of the interlocks.
